WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.880
Hardening must be verified.

00:00:02.100 --> 00:00:06.260
A network can look well controlled in documentation while reality has drifted.

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:11.920
Logs, alerts, configuration review, firewall rule review, access review,

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:17.980
vulnerability management, drift detection, availability monitoring, DNS monitoring,

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:19.560
certificate monitoring,

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.820
and periodic tabletop exercises all help teams understand whether controls still work.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:27.440
Monitoring turns assumptions into evidence.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:31.260
Logs and alerts should help teams detect unexpected exposure,

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.400
suspicious administrative access, failed changes, policy drift,

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:37.540
and operational degradation.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.200
Device logs can show authentication failures, configuration changes, routing instability,

00:00:43.420 --> 00:00:48.460
VPN anomalies, wireless issues, or firewall denies that indicate a broken dependency.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:53.240
Alerts should be meaningful enough that responders know what changed, what is affected,

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.300
and who owns the next step.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.160
Reviews catch the slow accumulation of risk.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:01.340
Firewall rules that were temporary become permanent.

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.120
Access control lists grow broader than intended.

00:01:04.340 --> 00:01:07.060
VPN groups gain users who no longer need them.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.840
DNS records point to retired services.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.060
Certificates approach expiration.

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:16.680
Configuration drift detection and periodic review make these issues visible before they

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:19.140
become incidents or outages.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:21.840
Continuous review improves the baseline.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:27.980
When an incident, outage, audit finding, or near miss occurs, ask what should change:

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:34.280
inventory, segmentation, access control, logging, alerting, patch process,

00:01:34.500 --> 00:01:35.860
or change review.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:38.960
Validation is not about proving the network is perfect.

00:01:39.180 --> 00:01:43.900
It is about confirming controls still match current risk and learning enough to make the

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:45.880
next hardening cycle better.
